[210.112118, "i", "cat <<'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline and Hardening Analysis Report\n\n**Host:** `smb-prod-01`  \n**Scope:** Security Baseline Configuration & Control Deficiencies  \n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nDuring the DFIR investigation of `smb-prod-01`, several critical configuration and baseline deficiencies were identified that allowed initial exploitation, enabled lateral movement/persistence, and inhibited rapid detection and incident response. Addressing these baseline weaknesses is mandatory to prevent recurrence.\n\n---\n\n## Baseline Deficiencies & Control Findings\n\n### 1. Legacy Protocol Enabled: SMBv1\n- **Check ID:** `CHK-07` / `BAS-01`\n- **Deficiency:** Legacy SMBv1 protocol driver `srv.sys` was enabled on the operating system, exposing port 445/tcp to untrusted remote networks.\n- **Impact:** Enabled unauthenticated remote exploitation via EternalBlue (CVE-2017-0144).\n- **Remediation Recommendation:** Disable SMBv1 feature completely via PowerShell (`Disable-WindowsOptionalFeature -Online -FeatureName SMB1Protocol`) or Group Policy, and block port 445 at the network perimeter.\n- **Evidence Citation:** `/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys` and `/sas/baseline.json`.\n\n### 2. Unmonitored WMI Event Subscriptions\n- **Deficiency:** Lack of monitoring or integrity checking on WMI repository objects permitted the attacker to create an `__EventFilter` and `CommandLineEventConsumer` without generating alerts.\n- **Impact:** Allowed silent, persistent privilege escalation and code execution across system reboots.\n- **Remediation Recommendation:** Deploy WMI auditing rules (Event ID 5861 in WMI-Activity operational log) and periodically audit `OBJECTS.DATA` or WMI namespaces via Sysmon / EDR.\n- **Evidence Citation:** `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`.\n\n### 3. Weak Directory Permissions & Insecure DLL Search Path\n- **Deficiency:** Sideloaded binary `wbemcomn.dll` was placed into `/Windows/System32/wbem/`.\n- **Impact:** Enables DLL search-order hijacking (`T1574.001`) where system applications load malicious DLLs prior to legitimate system libraries.\n- **Remediation Recommendation:** Enforce strict NTFS ACLs on System32 subdirectories, enable Safe DLL Search Mode, and implement EDR DLL loading rules.\n- **Evidence Citation:** `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`.\n\n### 4. Insufficient Event Log Forwarding & Anti-Forensic Resistance\n- **Deficiency:** Security event log was wiped by the attacker (Event ID 1102 recorded in `System.evtx`). System lacked real-time off-host syslog / SIEM event streaming.\n- **Impact:** Local log deletion blindfolded initial responder triage and concealed process creation event trails.\n- **Remediation Recommendation:** Implement real-time, tamper-resistant SIEM log forwarding via Winlogbeat / Syslog and alert instantly on Event ID 1102 / 104 log clear events.\n- **Evidence Citation:** `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`.\n\n### 5. Inadequate File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) & Endpoint Protection Coverage\n- **Deficiency:** EDR alerts (`/sas/alerts.json`) covered initial exploit binaries and credential dumps but failed to alert on registry backup modifications (`SECURITY.bak`), WMI subscriptions, or sideloaded DLLs.\n- **Impact:** Delayed identification of C2 infrastructure (`198.51.100.218`) and stealth persistence mechanisms.\n- **Remediation Recommendation:** Expand FIM rules to monitor WMI repositories, system DLL folders, and registry backup paths.\n- **Evidence Citation:** `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RegBack/SECURITY.bak` and `/sas/alerts.json`.\nEOF\n"]
